course: dictionary
lesson: 9291
title: Spat: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Spat» is the past form of «spit». Today he spits, yesterday he spat. Context tells you what it means. «Mia swapped salt for sugar; he spat it across the table» shows that spat means forced out of your mouth, because the tea went across the table, not into his stomach.
explain: A spat can also be a noun: a short, small argument. Look at «a spat with a friend». The word «with» tells you two people are involved, and the friend is probably someone you are cross with for a little while. Spats are soon over.
explain: Spat can describe angry words too. In «a spat-out word», the word was said suddenly and angrily, the way a shouted insult might sound. Check the clues: an angry face, a hard voice, a crowd going quiet. When you see these clues, your meaning fits the sentence.
fact: Spit is the base word; spat is its past form.
fact: «Spat» can be a verb (he spat) or a noun (a spat).
fact: «Spat out» means forced out of the mouth, often with force or anger.
fact: Spats (old-fashioned) are cloth covers worn over the top of shoes.
fact: Spitting is rude and spreads germs, so do not spit in public.
exercise: «Mia's brother spat the tea across the table.» What does spat mean here? | Poured it into a cup | Swallowed it quickly | *Forced it out of his mouth | Wiped it off the table
exercise: «Ella and Sam had a spat, but they were friends again by lunch.» What is a spat? | A long journey | A cloth cover for shoes | A type of sweet | *A short, small argument
exercise: Find and copy the word that is the past form of spit. | *spat | spitting | spits | spit
test: «You can tell a real insult by ear: an angry face, a spat-out word, a crowd going quiet.» What does a spat-out word suggest? | A word written on paper | A word that rhymes | A word said slowly and kindly | *A word said suddenly in anger
test: Gum spat on pavements costs towns a lot to clean. What does spat mean in this sentence? | Carefully stuck to a wall | Stretched into long strands | *Forced out of the mouth and dropped | Hidden under a bench
exam: Which sentence uses spat as a noun? | *Ella and Sam had a spat about the game. | The camel spat at the crowd. | Her brother spat angry words at the door. | Mia spat the tea across the table.
